    questionText: "Outline one economic argument in favour of and one economic argument against any two of the following government measures announced in Budget 2019: • Increase in social welfare payments of €5 per week • Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes • Introduction of 2 extra weeks paid parental leave to every parent of a child under one year of age.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"4 @ 6 (3 + 3)","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 24,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-favour", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An argument in favour — one for each of your two measures", marks: 12, contextNote: "One argument in favour of each measure you choose, 6 marks each (3 + 3). Each option names its measure.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":6,"options":["Increase in social welfare payments of €5 per week — Better standard of living More income is provided to the poorer sector so a better standard of living can be achieved. It may also compensate for the increase in the cost of living.","Increase in social welfare payments of €5 per week — Fairer distribution of Income The extra payment helps reduce the gap between rich and poor leading to a fairer distribution of income. May help reward those pensioners who contributed to the growth of the economy during their working lives.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Government revenue This increase will raise tax revenues for the government.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Reduce consumption / personal health Consumption of tobacco products may fall and health of consumers will improve. It may discourage younger people from smoking.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Social benefit A healthier society may lead to less absenteeism and more productivity.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Reduced healthcare costs As less people become ill there will be less demand for health services leading to lower health care costs.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Opportunity cost The money saved on healthcare can be put to some other use by the government.","Introduction of 2 extra weeks paid parental leave to every parent of a child under one year of age. — Some cost savings Reduced childcare costs / more disposable income.","Introduction of 2 extra weeks paid parental leave to every parent of a child under one year of age. — Social benefit Workers may be more contented and happier at work with more time at home and this may benefit employers and society at large.","Introduction of 2 extra weeks paid parental leave to every parent of a child under one year of age. — Increase in population May encourage an increase in the birth rate which will lead to a more productive age group paying tax in the future to finance pensions."]} },
    { id: "r-against", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An argument against — one for each of your two measures", marks: 12, contextNote: "One argument against each of the same two measures, 6 marks each (3 + 3).", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":6,"options":["Increase in social welfare payments of €5 per week — Increased Government expenditure / borrowing The government will spend more and so may require more borrowing to fund expenditure.","Increase in social welfare payments of €5 per week — Opportunity cost The money could be put to some alternative use e.g. to fund youth employment initiatives, education and training initiatives.","Increase in social welfare payments of €5 per week — Possibility of increased taxation With the increase in government expenditure the government may have to increase direct / indirect taxes to fund this increase.","Increase in social welfare payments of €5 per week — Disincentive to work An increase in social welfare payments may discourage people from entering the workforce.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Standard of living Disposable incomes will fall for smokers and this will lead to a lower standard of living.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Inflation Price of tobacco related products rise and this is included in the Consumer Price Index so the rate of inflation increases.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Inequitable / regressive Could be considered unfair as it applies to smokers.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Smuggling/black (shadow) economy may increase As cigarettes become more expensive more consumers may switch to smuggled cigarettes.","Increase of 50c in the price of a packet of 20 cigarettes — Tax revenue If consumption falls and/or smuggling increases then the tax revenue collected by the government may fall.","Introduction of 2 extra weeks paid parental leave to every parent of a child under one year of age. — Increased government expenditure Its provision must be paid for out of general taxation revenue and so taxes could increase.","Introduction of 2 extra weeks paid parental leave to every parent of a child under one year of age. — Opportunity cost The money used on parental leave could have been put to some other use by the government.","Introduction of 2 extra weeks paid parental leave to every parent of a child under one year of age. — Labour shortages Difficulty for employer in filling the temporary vacancies that arise."]} },
    ],
