    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2024 Ordinary Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2024-ol-q15-b-ii", topicId: "economics-1-0", conceptId: "explaining-the-fertiliser-supply-shift",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2024 OL Q15(b)(ii)",
    stem: "The diagram below shows the demand and supply curve for Irish farm output. The price of fertiliser in Ireland increased by 97.7% in the 12 months to October 2022. As a result, the increase in fertiliser prices has affected the supply of Irish farm output. Show the effect of this development on the diagram above.",
    questionText: "Explain your answer.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"9","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 9,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "• As the cost of fertiliser has risen many farmers have had to reduce its usage to cut expenses. • The fall in fertiliser use has resulted in lower crop yields on farms. • Consequently, the supply curve shifts left from S1 to S2 to reflect the fall in farm output. • There is now a new market equilibrium causing a new higher equilibrium price (Pe2) and • a new lower equilibrium quantity (Qe2).", marks: 9, contextNote: "Explain the chain, 9 marks: dearer fertiliser, less used, lower yields, so supply shifts left from S1 to S2, giving a higher equilibrium price (Pe2) and a lower equilibrium quantity (Qe2).\n\nIrish graph labels: É is demand; C/Pc/Cc correspond to English E/Pe/Qe. Explain the same leftward supply shift and higher price/lower quantity.", openList: true },
    ],
